{%- comment -%}
  oma-regen-practices — Regenerative Agriculture page, chapter 03 ("How
  regenerative agriculture actually works"): intro copy, optional
  illustration, then a sticky scroll-spy table of contents beside a column
  of <details> articles, one per practice block.

  A fresh section per the 2026-10-08 scope call (not built on oma-regen.liquid
  -- that section is the homepage teaser and has no TOC/scroll-spy), though
  the "practice" block shape below (title + richtext body) is deliberately
  the same shape as oma-regen's "point" block, since that part does fit.

  oma-regen-practices.js drives: which TOC item is "active" while scrolling,
  clicking a TOC item (open + smooth-scroll to it), and the expand/collapse-all
  button. Unlike oma-regen.js's homepage accordion, individual <details> here
  open/close natively (no animated height) -- this page already carries a lot
  of interaction; the plain native toggle keeps it legible.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% assign owid = section.id | replace: '-', '' | replace: '_', '' %}
{% style %}
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  background: {{ section.settings.background_color }};
  color: {{ section.settings.text_color }};
  padding: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px) clamp(20px, 4vw, 48px);
  font-family: var(--font-body-family);
  scroll-margin-top: 112px;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} *, .oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} *::before, .oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .inner {
  max-width: 1440px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .head {
  max-width: 900px;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .eyebrow {
  font: 800 12px/1.3 var(--font-heading-family);
  letter-spacing: .14em;
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} h2 {
  margin: 16px 0 0;
  font-family: var(--font-heading-family);
  font-weight: 800;
  font-size: clamp(34px, 4.4vw, 60px);
  line-height: .98;
  letter-spacing: -.04em;
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  text-wrap: balance;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .intro {
  margin: 20px 0 0;
  max-width: 780px;
  font-size: 14.5px;
  line-height: 1.6;
  font-weight: 500;
  text-wrap: pretty;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .illustration {
  margin: 40px auto 0;
  max-width: 1100px;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .illustration img {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  display: block;
  mix-blend-mode: multiply;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .illustration figcaption {
  margin-top: 12px;
  text-align: center;
  font-size: 12.5px;
  line-height: 1.55;
  font-weight: 500;
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-bar {
  display: none;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: 16px;
  margin-top: 48px;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toggle-all {
  border: 1.5px solid {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  background: none;
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  padding: 9px 14px;
  border-radius: 100px;
  font: 800 11px/1 var(--font-heading-family);
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background .15s ease, color .15s ease;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toggle-all:hover {
  background: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  color: {{ section.settings.background_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: 16px;
  align-items: start;
  margin-top: 16px;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} aside {
  display: none;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} aside .toc-head {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: 16px;
  height: 36px;
  margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-list {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  border-top: 1.5px solid {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-list li {
  border-bottom: 1.5px solid {{ section.settings.toc_rule_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-list li:last-child {
  border-bottom: none;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-link {
  width: 100%;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 28px minmax(0, 1fr) 12px;
  gap: 10px;
  align-items: center;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: 6px 10px;
  border: none;
  background: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  text-align: left;
  text-decoration: none;
  color: inherit;
  transition: background .15s ease;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-link:hover {
  background: {{ section.settings.hover_background }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-link .n {
  font: 800 11px/1 var(--font-heading-family);
  letter-spacing: .06em;
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-link .t {
  font: 600 14.5px/1.3 var(--font-heading-family);
  color: {{ section.settings.text_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-link .dot {
  width: 8px;
  height: 8px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: transparent;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-link[aria-current="true"] .t {
  font-weight: 800;
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-link[aria-current="true"] .dot {
  background: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .articles {
  min-width: 0;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} details {
  border-top: 1.5px solid {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  padding-bottom: 20px;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} summary {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 24px;
  gap: 16px;
  align-items: center;
  padding: 20px 0 0;
  cursor: pointer;
  list-style: none;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} summary::-webkit-details-marker {
  display: none;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} summary .head-inline {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: 16px;
  min-width: 0;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} summary .n {
  flex: none;
  width: 32px;
  font: 800 13px/1 var(--font-heading-family);
  letter-spacing: .08em;
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} summary h3 {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--font-heading-family);
  font-weight: 800;
  font-size: clamp(24px, 2.4vw, 34px);
  line-height: 1.05;
  letter-spacing: -.035em;
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  text-wrap: balance;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} summary .sign {
  justify-self: end;
  font: 800 22px/1 var(--font-heading-family);
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} summary .sign::after {
  content: '+';
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} details[open] summary .sign::after {
  content: '\2212';
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .answer {
  padding-left: 0;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .answer > * {
  margin: 16px 0 0;
  font-size: 14.5px;
  line-height: 1.6;
  font-weight: 500;
  color: {{ section.settings.text_color }};
  text-wrap: pretty;
  }
.oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} :focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid currentColor;
  outline-offset: 4px;
  }
@media (min-width: 990px) {
  .oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-bar {
    display: none;
  }
  .oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .layout {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(240px, 1fr) minmax(0, 2fr);
    gap: 64px;
    margin-top: 48px;
  }
  .oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} aside {
    display: block;
    position: sticky;
    top: 112px;
    }
  .oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .answer {
    padding-left: 48px;
  }
}
@media (max-width: 989px) {
  .oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }} .toc-bar {
    display: flex;
  }
}
{% endstyle %}
<div id="regen-how" class="oma-regen-practices-{{ owid }}" data-oma-regen-practices data-expand-label="{{ section.settings.expand_label | escape }}" data-collapse-label="{{ section.settings.collapse_label | escape }}">
  <div class="inner">
    <div class="head">
      {%- if section.settings.eyebrow != blank -%}<div class="eyebrow">{{ section.settings.eyebrow | escape }}</div>{%- endif -%}
      <h2>{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>
      {%- if section.settings.intro != blank -%}<div class="intro">{{ section.settings.intro }}</div>{%- endif -%}
    </div>
    {%- if section.settings.illustration != blank -%}
      <figure class="illustration">
        {{ section.settings.illustration | image_url: width: 1600 | image_tag: loading: 'lazy', widths: '750, 1100, 1600', sizes: '(min-width: 1100px) 1100px, 100vw' }}
        {%- if section.settings.illustration_caption != blank -%}
          <figcaption>{{ section.settings.illustration_caption | escape }}</figcaption>
        {%- endif -%}
      </figure>
    {%- endif -%}
    <div class="toc-bar">
      <span class="eyebrow">{{ section.settings.toc_label | escape }}</span>
      <button type="button" class="toggle-all" data-toggle-all>{{ section.settings.collapse_label | escape }}</button>
    </div>
    <div class="layout">
      <aside>
        <div class="toc-head">
          <span class="eyebrow">{{ section.settings.toc_label | escape }}</span>
          <button type="button" class="toggle-all" data-toggle-all>{{ section.settings.collapse_label | escape }}</button>
        </div>
        <ol class="toc-list">
          {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
            <li>
              <a class="toc-link" href="#regen-practice-{{ forloop.index }}" data-toc-link data-target="regen-practice-{{ forloop.index }}" aria-current="{% if forloop.first %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}">
                <span class="n">{% if forloop.index < 10 %}0{% endif %}{{ forloop.index }}</span>
                <span class="t">{{ block.settings.title | escape }}</span>
                <span class="dot"></span>
              </a>
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <div class="articles">
        {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
          <details id="regen-practice-{{ forloop.index }}" open data-practice {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
            <summary>
              <span class="head-inline">
                <span class="n">{% if forloop.index < 10 %}0{% endif %}{{ forloop.index }}</span>
                <h3>{{ block.settings.title | escape }}</h3>
              </span>
              <span class="sign"></span>
            </summary>
            <div class="answer">{{ block.settings.body }}</div>
          </details>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<script src="{{ 'oma-regen-practices.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
{% schema %}
{
  "name": "OMA Regen Practices",
  "tag": "section",
  "disabled_on": {
    "groups": ["header", "footer"]
  },
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow", "default": "03 · THE PRACTICES" },
    { "type": "inline_richtext", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "How regenerative agriculture actually works" },
    { "type": "richtext", "id": "intro", "label": "Intro paragraph", "default": "<p>Regenerative agriculture begins from a different premise. Instead of asking what can be taken from the land, it starts with what the land has, and what it needs. The practices that follow are not isolated techniques, but parts of one interconnected system, each reinforcing the next. Together, they rebuild a living universe, and given enough time, the land begins to sustain itself.</p>" },
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "illustration", "label": "Illustration (optional)" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "illustration_caption", "label": "Illustration caption", "default": "How nature works 101 — for the distracted modern human." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "toc_label", "label": "Table-of-contents label", "default": "SEVEN PRACTICES" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "expand_label", "label": "\"Expand all\" button label", "default": "Expand all" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "collapse_label", "label": "\"Collapse all\" button label", "default": "Collapse all" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Colors" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "background_color", "label": "Background", "default": "#F7F5F1" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "accent_color", "label": "Accent (eyebrow, headings, numbers)", "default": "#FF3F00" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "text_color", "label": "Body text", "default": "#00180C" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "toc_rule_color", "label": "TOC divider", "default": "#FFE4E7" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "hover_background", "label": "TOC item hover background", "default": "#FFE4E7" }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "name": "Practice",
      "settings": [
        { "type": "text", "id": "title", "label": "Title" },
        { "type": "richtext", "id": "body", "label": "Body" }
      ],
      "limit": 10
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "OMA Regen Practices",
      "blocks": [
        { "type": "practice", "settings": { "title": "Cover Cropping", "body": "<p>In conventional farming, fields are left to rest and rejuvenate between growing seasons. When soil is left bare and exposed, it is vulnerable to erosion, compaction, and nutrient loss. Covering land with fast-growing plant species (i.e. cover cropping) protects and nourishes the ground even when nothing is being harvested.</p><p>Some cover crops fix nitrogen from the air directly into the soil, reducing or eliminating the need for synthetic fertilisers. Others attract beneficial insects, suppress weeds naturally, or add organic matter to feed the ground below.</p><p>But the assumption that cover crops are non cash crops, meaning lost income, isn't always true. Oats, barley, peas and certain legumes can generate income while rebuilding soil fertility. In a well-designed regenerative system, the line between a cover crop and cash crop begins to blur.</p><p>A farmer transitioning from conventional to regenerative agriculture doesn't necessarily have to give up income during the transition period if they choose dual-purpose cover crops intentionally. But this points to a bigger inquiry: if keeping the soil covered between harvests makes such a difference, what happens when you never have to leave it bare at all?</p>" } },
        { "type": "practice", "settings": { "title": "Polyculture + Intercropping", "body": "<p>That question leads to polyculture farming where multiple species are grown together rather than one monocrop across an entire field. While monoculture farming is efficient to manage and easy to mechanise, it is also a deeply unnatural process. No ecosystem on earth thrives in monoculture. Nature favours diversity because it acts as a superior defence against weather, disease, and crop failures.</p><p>In a polyculture system, each species serves a purpose. Some fix nitrogen that others consume. Some develop deep root systems that drink and distribute water while breaking up compacted soil for shallow root neighbours. Some attract the insects and pollinators that the whole system depends on. In a mature polyculture system, land is rarely bare and interrupted. Each crop cycle leaves the soil slightly richer than it found it, while building toward something more complex than what came before.</p>" } },
        { "type": "practice", "settings": { "title": "No-Till Farming", "body": "<p>Imagine plowing the soil with heavy machinery before every planting cycle. The land gets turned over, but each time soil is tilled, the fungal networks and microbial communities living within it are destroyed. These networks, some stretching kilometres underground, are the channels required to move nutrients and water through the soil. Without them, the land becomes dependent on synthetic inputs to do what nature was already doing for free.</p><p>No-till farming leaves everything intact. Seeds are planted directly into undisturbed ground, preserving the living ecosystem beneath the surface. Over time, the system strengthens and self-sustains naturally: organic matter accumulates, water retention improves, and soil fertility builds.</p><p>In a polyculture system, no-till is preferred and inevitable. A field growing multiple crops at different heights, root depths and harvest times cannot be mechanically ploughed without destroying everything. The complexity of a polyculture system protects itself.</p>" } },
        { "type": "practice", "settings": { "title": "No Synthetic Chemicals", "body": "<p>The biological diversity built by polyculture and no-till farming is undone the moment synthetic chemicals are introduced. Synthetic herbicides, pesticides and fertilisers are designed to kill weeds, eliminate pests, and replace lost nutrients. But they can't tell the difference between good and bad. Much like antibiotics, they wipe out entire organisms, including the beneficial ones a healthy ecosystem depends on.</p><p>Herbicides clear weeds but disrupt the root relationships that hold soil communities together. Pesticides eliminate harmful pests but suppress the bees, butterflies and pollinators the whole system relies on. Synthetic fertilisers feed crops but shift soil pH and sever the plant-fungi exchange that builds long-term fertility, meaning more than half the nitrogen applied never even reaches the plant, instead leaching into waterways.</p><p>Consequences compound. Chemical residues accumulate in soil, water, plants, animals and humans. Crops and weeds grow increasingly resistant, demanding stronger applications to achieve the same results. Pollinator populations decline. Biodiversity collapses, not just in plant species but in birds and mammals. The more chemicals a farm uses, the more it relies on them — and the harder it becomes to stop.</p>" } },
        { "type": "practice", "settings": { "title": "Composting", "body": "<p>If synthetic chemicals represent the extractive logic of conventional farming, composting represents the opposite: it returns what was taken. Crop residues, tree cuttings, leaves, food scraps, and animal manure, recycles organic matter back into the soil. What most consider waste is actually food for an entire colony underground: microbes, fungi, and earthworms.</p>" } },
        { "type": "practice", "settings": { "title": "Integrated Grazing", "body": "<p>For most of agricultural history, animals and farming were inseparable. They graze and stimulate grass growth, their movement aerates the soil, and their manure feeds colonies below.</p><p>In industrial farming, livestock is often removed from the fields and concentrated in feedlots. Their manure becomes a waste problem rather than a source of contribution. Instead, farmers purchase synthetic fertilisers to replace what had been provided free.</p><p>Regenerative farming reintegrates them thoughtfully. Strategic grazing moves herds across the pasture in patterns that mimic wild grazing, giving land time to recover between visits. Graze too long in one place and the soil compacts and vegetation decreases. Graze at the right time and everything benefits.</p>" } },
        { "type": "practice", "settings": { "title": "Agroforestry", "body": "<p>Just as animals can be deliberately integrated into agricultural systems, so can trees.</p><p>Mature agroforestry systems don't look like conventional farms. They resemble forests — designed to mimic them by integrating trees and shrubs that create canopy cover, moderate temperature, reduce wind, and shelter life below.</p><p>Agroforestry systems host three to five times more bird species and beneficial insects than monoculture fields, bringing natural pest control, pollination, and organic matter that benefits the entire ecosystem. Their deep root systems stabilise slopes, prevent land erosion, and draw water up from far below the surface. Their fallen leaves become mulch and compost, returning nutrients to the soil that are drawn back up by the very plants and trees that shed them.</p>" } }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
